            <ArticleTitle>[Results of gastric bypass for morbid obesity after a follow up of seven to 10 years].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1414-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">/S0034-98872011001100004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">There is a paucity of information about the long term effects of gastric bypass for morbid obesity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To study the evolution of weight and complications of obesity, seven to 10 years after gastric bypass surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred eighteen subjects with morbid obesity, aged 15 to 66 years (103 women), were followed for a mean of 94 months after surgery. Body weight, fasting blood glucose, total cholesterol, triglycerides and hemoglobin were measured before surgery and during follow up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">At 24 months of follow up, all patients lost weight and there was a mild weight increase at 94 months, that paralleled the preoperative body mass index. Diabetes, hypercholesterolemia and hypertriglyceridemia subsided in 95, 87 and 94% of cases, respectively. Twenty percent of patients had mild anemia and 11% moderate or severe anemia. No patient recovered the preoperative weight.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Weight reducing effects of gastric bypass are maintained after 94 months of follow up with the expected health benefits.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
